  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Dry and mild weather through midweek, next \nsystem to arrive on Thursday followed by coldest air of the \nseason this weekend.\n\nHigh pressure will continue to dominate the synoptic weather \npattern for the next several days, keeping dry and mild weather \nin place for the Mid-Atlantic. As the high shifts east into the \nAtlantic for the early part of the week, high temperatures will \nrise to around 10-15 degrees above normal for this time of year,\nin the 60s to low 70s for Tuesday and Wednesday.\n\nThe next chance of precipitation will arrive on Thursday, as a \nlongwave upper-level trough and a surface low pressure system \ndevelops across the Mississippi Valley midweek and track \nnortheast into the Ohio River Valley on Thursday. Models have \nremained fairly consistent with the strength of the trough, \nencompassing most of the Eastern CONUS Friday into next weekend.\nThe system will bring a chance of rainfall to the area, but \namounts are trending lighter for our region. \n\nOnce the front moves through late Thursday, a much colder \nairmass will arrive for Friday and into Saturday. Wraparound \nmoisture will likely give the western slopes their first upslope\nsnow event of the season given a strong northwest flow moisture\nfetch. Too early to discuss snowfall amounts, but an \naccumulating snow is becoming more of a possibility in the \nhighest elevations of far southwest VA and southeast WV.",
